<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Building the trapezoidal rule from trapezia</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>A region is split into \({@n@}\) strips, each \({@hh@}\,\text{m}\) wide. The ordinates (the heights at the edges of the strips), in metres, are \(y_0, y_1, \ldots, y_{ {@n@} }\).</p><p>Type y0, y1 and so on for the ordinates, for example <code>3*(y0+y1)</code>.</p><p>(a) Write an expression for the area of strip \({@kk@}\), treated as a trapezium.</p><div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) Add the areas of all \({@n@}\) trapezia. Write the total as one expression in \(y_0\) to \(y_{ {@n@} }\).</p><div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) Strip \({@kk@}\) lies between the ordinates \(y_{ {@kk-1@} }\) and \(y_{ {@kk@} }\). A trapezium has area \(\tfrac{1}{2} \times\) (sum of the parallel sides) \(\times\) width, so its area is \({@ta1@}\).</p><p>(b) The areas of the \({@n@}\) strips are \({@tsum@}\). Add them. Each inner ordinate is a side of two trapezia, so it is counted twice, while \(y_0\) and \(y_{ {@n@} }\) appear once:</p><p>\[A \approx {@ta2@}\]</p><p>This is the trapezoidal rule \(A \approx \tfrac{1}{2}h\left[y_0 + y_n + 2(y_1 + y_2 + \cdots + y_{n-1})\right]\) with \(h = {@hh@}\) and \(n = {@n@}\).</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>2</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <idnumber></idnumber>
  <stackversion><text>2026063000</text></stackversion>
  <questionvariables>
    <text><![CDATA[validate_y7(ex) := if every(lambda([v], member(v, [y0,y1,y2,y3,y4,y5,y6,y7])), listofvars(ex)) then "" else "Use only the ordinates y0, y1, y2, ... in your expression.";
/* Design: the sum of trapezia collected into the trapezoidal rule; n strips with a numeric h. Entries [n, h, k]; h is even, so h/2 is whole. Source: adapted from SMP AS/A2 Core 2, Chapter 9, Section C. */
L: [[3,2,2],[4,10,3],[5,4,2],[6,6,4],[4,8,2],[3,20,3],[5,10,5],[6,2,3],[4,6,4],[5,4,3]];
ix: 1+rand(10);
n: L[ix][1]; hh: L[ix][2]; kk: L[ix][3];
YA: [y0,y1,y2,y3,y4,y5,y6,y7];
yv: makelist(YA[j+1],j,0,n);
ta1: hh/2*(yv[kk]+yv[kk+1]);
inner: apply("+",rest(rest(yv),-1));
ta2: hh/2*(first(yv)+last(yv)+2*inner);
tsum: makelist(hh/2*(yv[j]+yv[j+1]),j,1,n);
/* diagnostics */
d1h: hh*(yv[kk]+yv[kk+1]);
d1r: hh*yv[kk+1]; d1l: hh*yv[kk];
d1s: if kk < n then hh/2*(yv[kk+1]+yv[kk+2]) else hh/2*(yv[kk-1]+yv[kk]);
d2h: hh*(first(yv)+last(yv)+2*inner);
d2once: hh/2*apply("+",yv);
d2all: hh*apply("+",yv);
d2ends: hh/2*(2*first(yv)+2*last(yv)+inner);
d2rect: hh*apply("+",rest(yv,-1));
/* underscore test inputs (consolidatesubscripts) */
us: [y0=y_0,y1=y_1,y2=y_2,y3=y_3,y4=y_4,y5=y_5,y6=y_6];]]></text>
  </questionvariables>
